  "headline": "Markets price oil-led inflation and tighter policy, not a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "The configuration reads as inflationary reflation: nominal growth is running hot, energy is driving the price pressure, and the market is pricing a policy response. Brent at $107 is up 26% over three months and 76% year-to-date, the 10-year yield sits at 5.22% after a 105 basis point rise this year, and the dollar index is within a whisker of its 52-week high after a 3.1% gain over the month. That is a rates-and-inflation story, not fear.",
    "The interplay confirms it. Gold has fallen 4.9% over the month while the 10-year climbed 24 basis points, the signature of rising real yields rather than a safety bid. Copper at 91% of its yearly range and a copper/gold ratio up 7.9% over the month say growth is intact. The 30-year/10-year spread has flattened 3 basis points to 35 as the 10-year leads the move, the shape produced when the market prices tighter policy for longer. Bitcoin, up 34% over three months in a calm volatility regime and buying 13% more gold than a month ago, says risk appetite is alive despite higher rates.",
    "The tensions are at the edges. A dollar this strong usually accompanies tighter financial conditions, and USD/CAD at a 52-week high despite $107 oil shows the rate differential, not the commodity, is in charge. The rupee's 1.6% jump today is a genuine outlier against that backdrop. The weight of evidence sits with the oil-yields-dollar complex: this is an economy the market judges too warm, not too cold."
  ],
